WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:07.770
You ever feel frustrated when a show wraps
up before the long summer hiatus and leaves

00:00:07.770 --> 00:00:08.969
things unresolved?

00:00:08.969 --> 00:00:11.429
Hey, it's just a season finale.

00:00:11.429 --> 00:00:14.209
You'll get closure when that show returns
in the fall.

00:00:14.209 --> 00:00:19.330
But then there are some long-running TV shows
that ended a season on their cliffhanger—and

00:00:19.330 --> 00:00:21.310
then got canceled.

00:00:21.310 --> 00:00:26.000
Here are some shows where audiences never
got to find out what happened.

00:00:26.000 --> 00:00:27.109
Mork and Mindy

00:00:27.109 --> 00:00:32.160
Elevated by the staggering comic gifts of
Robin Williams, this show's four-season run

00:00:32.160 --> 00:00:35.100
came to an end in a truly weird fashion.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:41.460
An evil alien named Kelnik tries to kill Mork
and Mindy, but they escape using a pair of

00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:43.980
magical time-travelling shoes.

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:44.980
But oops!

00:00:44.980 --> 00:00:48.530
They're damaged, and the pair go too far back
in time.

00:00:48.530 --> 00:00:51.440
The last image: a cave painting of Mork and
Mindy.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:53.780
Did they ever get back to the future?

00:00:53.780 --> 00:00:57.050
We'll never know, because the show got canceled.

00:00:57.050 --> 00:00:58.050
Sliders

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.740
On the final installment of Sliders, a show
featuring a cast of dimension-hopping characters,

00:01:02.740 --> 00:01:08.150
a psychic tells the crew that if they slide
through space and time just once more, they'll

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:09.150
die.

00:01:09.150 --> 00:01:14.080
However, they find a loophole: if one of them
slides but also injects themselves with a

00:01:14.080 --> 00:01:16.010
special tonic, they'll be okay.

00:01:16.010 --> 00:01:19.110
The character Rembrandt does it and slides
on through.

00:01:19.110 --> 00:01:20.260
Does he live?

00:01:20.260 --> 00:01:21.260
Does he die?

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:26.530
The psychic could tell the others, but while
fighting off the team's enemies, she dies.

00:01:26.530 --> 00:01:27.550
Freaks and Geeks

00:01:27.550 --> 00:01:31.910
Freaks and Geeks made stars out of cast members
like James Franco and Seth Rogen.

00:01:31.910 --> 00:01:36.460
But when this show aired in 1999, it finished
near the bottom of the ratings.

00:01:36.460 --> 00:01:40.560
NBC canceled it before all 18 episodes were
broadcast.

00:01:40.560 --> 00:01:44.740
One of the season's story arcs followed Lindsay
Weir and her struggles with whether or not

00:01:44.740 --> 00:01:48.299
to remain a hard-working, school-first good
girl.

00:01:48.299 --> 00:01:52.670
She ultimately ditches an academic summit
to go to a Grateful Dead concert.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:57.850
At the end, Lindsay hops in a van, and it
drives away…and we never know if Lindsay

00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.130
made it there, or back.

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.700
My Name is Earl

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.369
This sitcom followed around an amiable dirtbag
named Earl, played by Jason Lee, who wins

00:02:07.369 --> 00:02:11.829
the lottery and spends his life making amends
for all the bad things he's done.

00:02:11.829 --> 00:02:18.209
Despite being a big hit for NBC, it was abruptly
canceled at the end of season four in 2009.

00:02:18.209 --> 00:02:24.040
The last scene drops a bombshell: Earl's actually
the father of his ex-wife's baby, and she's

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:25.319
about to explain!

00:02:25.319 --> 00:02:31.200
But the episode—and series—ends with a
"To Be Continued" graphic.

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.200
"NBC?"

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.939
"That's for canceling My Name is Earl!"

00:02:37.939 --> 00:02:41.060
Lois and Clark: The New Adventures of Superman

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:44.010
Each decade gets the Superman it deserves.

00:02:44.010 --> 00:02:49.980
Sad, post-Watergate '70s America got happy-positive
Christopher Reeve, and superficial, Melrose

00:02:49.980 --> 00:02:56.359
Place-loving '90s America got this soapy super-show
starring Dean Cain and Teri Hatcher.

00:02:56.359 --> 00:03:01.079
Season four ended with a super-seeming baby
left on Lois and Clark's doorstep.

00:03:01.079 --> 00:03:02.760
Was it from Krypton like Superman?

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:03.840
Who knows?

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:08.129
ABC decided a fifth season wasn’t such a
super idea.

00:03:08.129 --> 00:03:09.129
Angel

00:03:09.129 --> 00:03:14.590
Toward the end of its run, Angel and his gang
take on the evil law firm of Wolfram and Hart.

00:03:14.590 --> 00:03:19.389
The final scene of season five shows the protagonists
preparing to storm their enemies.

00:03:19.389 --> 00:03:23.590
The show's creative team didn't intend for
this to be the ending, but they learned too

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:28.769
late that they weren't getting renewed, so
they ended the show on a cliffhanger.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:33.620
But creator Joss Whedon is okay with that,
satisfied it suggests the fight against evil

00:03:33.620 --> 00:03:35.489
never truly ends.

00:03:35.489 --> 00:03:36.489
Thanks for watching!

00:03:36.489 --> 00:03:40.230
Subscribe to our YouTube channel to watch
more videos like the one you just saw.

00:03:40.230 --> 00:03:44.159
And leave us a comment to let us know which
cliffhanger frustrated you most.

